# Architecture

This project is a Rust TUI built with hexagonal architecture and a DDD-flavoured
domain. There is no indexer; every piece of on-chain data is obtained live from an
external API.

## Layers

- **Domain** (`src/domain/`): pure types and invariants. No I/O, no serialization,
  no async. Contains value objects (`Address`, `TxHash`, `BlockNumber`, `Chain`,
  `Wei`, `Gwei`, `UnixTimestamp`, ...), entities (`Block`, `Transaction`,
  `Account`, `Contract`, `TokenMetadata`, ...), and `DomainError`.
- **Application** (`src/application/`): orchestration. Defines ports as `trait`s
  under `ports/` and implements use cases under `use_cases/`. Use cases depend on
  ports, never on concrete adapters.
- **Adapters** (`src/adapters/`): concrete implementations of ports. Owns HTTP /
  JSON-RPC clients, serde types, retry policies, and maps raw responses into
  domain types.
- **Infra** (`src/infra/`): composition root. Loads config, constructs adapters,
  wires them into use cases, boots the Tokio runtime, starts the TUI loop.

## Module boundary rules

- `domain` imports nothing internal.
- `application` imports `domain` only.
- `adapters::*` import `domain` and `application`.
- `adapters::ui` must not import other `adapters::*`. The UI depends on the
  application through ports passed in by `infra`.
- `infra` is the only module that may import everything.

Violations are review-blockers. Prefer `cargo-modules` and targeted `use`
discipline over runtime dependency injection frameworks.

## Dependency inversion

Use cases receive their dependencies as generic bounds or `Arc<dyn PortTrait>`.
They never construct concrete clients.

```rust
pub struct LoadTxOverview<R, S, D>
where
    R: TxReaderPort,
    S: ContractSourcePort,
    D: SignatureDirectoryPort,
{
    reader: R,
    sources: S,
    signatures: D,
}
```

Prefer generics for performance-critical hot paths, `Arc<dyn ...>` for screens
that need to carry ports around as runtime state.

## DDD primitives

- Value objects are newtypes with validation in their constructor:

  ```rust
  pub struct Address([u8; 20]);

  impl Address {
      pub fn from_hex(s: &str) -> Result<Self, DomainError> { /* ... */ }
  }
  ```

- Never leak primitive types (`String`, `u64`, `[u8; N]`) into application APIs
  when a newtype exists.
- All fallible constructors return `Result<Self, DomainError>`; infallible
  constructors use `From`.
- Equality and ordering are explicit (`#[derive(PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]` only when
  the domain semantics are that strict).

## No indexer

- No SQLite, no RocksDB, no custom indexer, no event-sourced persistence for
  on-chain data. MVP stays external-only.
- Cache is allowed, but it must be in-memory and TTL-based (`adapters/cache/`).
- Persistence is limited to the config file and, later, a small key-value store
  for UI preferences.

## Error model

- Domain-level errors are `DomainError` variants defined in
  `src/domain/errors.rs`.
- Adapter-level errors are `thiserror` enums local to each adapter module; they
  are mapped into `DomainError` at the application boundary.
- Use `Result<T, DomainError>` in all port signatures.

## Runtime

- Single Tokio multi-thread runtime, owned by `infra`.
- UI task never blocks on I/O. Data is produced by use-case tasks and delivered
  through `tokio::sync::mpsc` channels.
- Subscriptions are owned by screen-scoped tasks and dropped when the screen is
  popped from the stack.
